I recognize this as Marcus et al. (1999), the ABA vs. ABB rule-learning study with 7-month-olds. Replications have been mixed. Rabagliati, Ferguson & Lew-Williams (2019) ran a meta-analysis and found a small to moderate effect for the novelty preference, though the evidence was weaker for the underlying rule learning. The Many Babies-style and other direct replications have had mixed results. Some labs replicated the original and others did not.

This claim is the novelty preference: infants attend longer to unfamiliar structures. The original sample was only 16, and the effect was reported as large. In a replication, the original design (Experiment 1) seems to have been used, possibly with a larger sample. Replication projects such as the Many Labs-style SCORE replications may have found a significant effect, since a larger sample can detect a modest effect. Even so, a small true effect makes a significant result uncertain. My estimate is about 0.5.

