The study is Stahlberg, Sczesny & Braun (2001) on generic masculine in German. Participants named favorite musicians, and the main effect of participant sex shows that women named more female musicians than men did. The effect was F(1,84) = 19.9, which corresponds to r of about 0.43. That is a robust effect and probably replicable. Women naming more women than men do is plausible in a replication. Whether the replication shares the same language and context is uncertain, since it could be a German-language replication or an English one. The sex-of-participant effect is a same-gender preference effect that tends to replicate well. The sample is small, but the evidence is strong.

I do not recall the specific replication outcome.


