I recognize this as Vohs & Schooler (2008), Study 1. The original had a small sample (N=30) and a moderate-to-large effect. Later replications of the free-will-belief manipulation on cheating mostly failed. Examples are the Many Labs-style efforts, Open Science Collaboration-type attempts, and Nature Human Behaviour-style multi-site work on determinism priming. The Velez & Hanus-type replications and the Open Science Framework's Registered Replication Report on the free-will/cheating link found null effects. Prior plausibility is low, and the small sample suggests an inflated effect. That said, the claim could have been replicated in a project where the replication used a larger sample, but the overall evidence points to failure.


